        {
          "id": "7493196911932428288",
          "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7493196911932428288",
          "title": "Flaky tests aren't bad luck. They're one of five things and all five are fixable.\n\nAfter years of hunting flakiness, it's almost always on this list:\n \n1. Hard waits. `waitForTimeout(3000)` is a bet that the app is ready in 3 seconds. Sometimes you lose. Use web-first assertions that auto-retry instead.\n\n2. Racing the network. The test acts before data arrives. Wait for the *state* you need, not a guessed delay.\n   \n3. Shared state. Test B depends on what test A left behind. Isolate every test. Assume nothing carries over.\n\n4. Fragile locators. Index-based or deeply-nested selectors break on the smallest UI change. Prefer role- and text-based locators.\n  \n5. The environment. Animations, CI machines under load, slow cold starts. Real, but usually the smallest slice once 1–4 are fixed.\n  \n#TestAutomation #Playwright #QA",
